1
00:00:04,485 --> 00:00:13,015
Work a denial to resolution — Billing ▸ Denials — Read the reason code, fix the cause, and appeal — money you already earned.

2
00:00:18,321 --> 00:00:26,683
Billing ▸ Denials is where refused claims land. Every row here is work you have already done and not been paid for.

3
00:00:26,683 --> 00:00:40,380
Treat it as a worklist, not an archive. Denials have filing deadlines — an untouched queue quietly becomes written-off revenue.

4
00:00:40,381 --> 00:00:44,764
Start from the patient, then the claim that was refused.

5
00:00:44,764 --> 00:00:53,173
Picking the claim pulls its payer and amount across — the denial stays tied to the claim it came from.

6
00:00:53,173 --> 00:01:02,001
The reason code is the payer telling you exactly what is wrong. CO-16 means the claim is missing information.

7
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:16,654
Learn the common ones and the fix is obvious: CO-16 add what is missing, CO-45 the charge exceeded the fee schedule, CO-18 it is a duplicate.

8
00:01:16,654 --> 00:01:29,399
Priority is what makes the queue sort itself — high-value and near-deadline first.

9
00:01:29,399 --> 00:01:40,827
Write what you actually did. The next person to touch this — or you in three weeks — needs the trail.

10
00:01:40,827 --> 00:01:46,739
Recorded, and it joins the queue as open with an appeal status of not started.

11
00:01:46,739 --> 00:01:56,061
Fix the cause, resubmit, and move it to resolved. A denial worked is revenue recovered; a denial ignored is revenue lost.

12
00:01:57,697 --> 00:02:17,607
What you just did

13
00:02:18,214 --> 00:02:27,214
Health | Efficiency | Growth — More in the Getting Started series — the next video is linked on screen.
